§§ `s 90`

`a free and informed consent` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS FALSE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS FALSE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS FALSE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS FALSE
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS FALSE
#ASSERT `consent within section 90` `a free and informed consent`

-- s 90(a)(i): under fear of injury, known to the doer.
`consent under fear of injury, known to the doer` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS TRUE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS TRUE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS FALSE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS FALSE
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `consent within section 90` `consent under fear of injury, known to the doer`

-- s 90(a)(ii): a misconception of fact the doer neither knew nor had reason to
-- believe of does not vitiate ("and the person doing the act knows, or has
-- reason to believe").
`consent under a misconception unknown to the doer` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS FALSE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS TRUE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS FALSE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS FALSE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS FALSE
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS FALSE
#ASSERT `consent within section 90` `consent under a misconception unknown to the doer`

-- s 90(b)
`consent by a person too intoxicated to understand` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS FALSE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS FALSE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS TRUE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS FALSE
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `consent within section 90` `consent by a person too intoxicated to understand`

-- s 90(c), and "unless the contrary appears from the context"
`consent by a child of 11` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS FALSE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS FALSE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS FALSE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS `below 12 years of age` 11
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `consent within section 90` `consent by a child of 11`

`consent by a child of 11 where the context shows otherwise` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS FALSE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS FALSE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS FALSE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS `below 12 years of age` 11
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `consent within section 90` `consent by a child of 11 where the context shows otherwise`

`consent by a person of 12` MEANS `Consent Facts` WITH
    `consent was given` IS TRUE
    `given under fear of injury or wrongful restraint to the person or to some other person` IS FALSE
    `given under a misconception of fact` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act knows, or has reason to believe, that the consent was given in consequence of such fear or misconception` IS FALSE
    `given by a person unable to understand the nature and consequence of that to which he gives his consent` IS FALSE
    `given by a person who is below 12 years of age` IS `below 12 years of age` 12
    `the contrary appears from the context` IS FALSE
#ASSERT `consent within section 90` `consent by a person of 12`

§§ `s 92`

-- s 92 illus (a): Z is thrown from his horse and insensible; A, a surgeon,
-- not intending Z's death but in good faith for Z's benefit, trepans him
-- before he recovers. No offence.
`trepanning the insensible rider` MEANS `Benefit Without Consent Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person` IS TRUE
    `done in good faith for the benefit of that person` IS TRUE
    `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent` IS TRUE
    `that person is incapable of giving consent` IS FALSE
    `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit` IS TRUE
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death` IS TRUE
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death` IS TRUE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` IS TRUE
    `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt` IS FALSE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt` IS TRUE
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `exception under s 92` `trepanning the insensible rider`

-- s 92 illus (c): a surgeon operates at once on a child whose accident is
-- likely to prove fatal; there is no time to apply to the guardian; the child
-- protests. No offence.
`operating on a child with no time to reach the guardian` MEANS `Benefit Without Consent Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person` IS TRUE
    `done in good faith for the benefit of that person` IS TRUE
    `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent` IS FALSE
    `that person is incapable of giving consent` IS TRUE
    `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit` IS TRUE
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death` IS TRUE
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death` IS TRUE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` IS TRUE
    `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt` IS FALSE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt` IS TRUE
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `exception under s 92` `operating on a child with no time to reach the guardian`

-- s 92 illus (d): A drops a child from a burning house-top into a blanket,
-- knowing the fall may kill it, not intending to, in good faith for its
-- benefit. No offence even if the child dies.
`dropping a child from a burning house` MEANS `Benefit Without Consent Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person` IS TRUE
    `done in good faith for the benefit of that person` IS TRUE
    `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent` IS FALSE
    `that person is incapable of giving consent` IS TRUE
    `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit` IS TRUE
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death` IS TRUE
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death` IS FALSE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` IS TRUE
    `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt` IS FALSE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt` IS TRUE
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `exception under s 92` `dropping a child from a burning house`

-- "has no guardian ... from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time":
-- with the guardian at hand, s 92 does not apply.
`operating on a child whose guardian could be asked in time` MEANS `Benefit Without Consent Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person` IS TRUE
    `done in good faith for the benefit of that person` IS TRUE
    `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent` IS FALSE
    `that person is incapable of giving consent` IS TRUE
    `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit` IS FALSE
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death` IS TRUE
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death` IS TRUE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` IS TRUE
    `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt` IS FALSE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt` IS TRUE
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend` IS TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `exception under s 92` `operating on a child whose guardian could be asked in time`

-- proviso (c): hurt for a purpose other than preventing death or hurt.
`hurting an insensible person to cure a minor ailment` MEANS `Benefit Without Consent Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm which it may cause to that person` IS TRUE
    `done in good faith for the benefit of that person` IS TRUE
    `the circumstances are such that it is impossible for that person to signify consent` IS TRUE
    `that person is incapable of giving consent` IS FALSE
    `has no guardian or other person in lawful charge of him from whom it is possible to obtain consent in time for the thing to be done with benefit` IS TRUE
    `not the intentional causing of death, or the attempting to cause death` IS TRUE
    `not the doing of anything which the doer knows to be likely to cause death` IS TRUE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or grievous hurt, or curing a grievous disease or infirmity` IS FALSE
    `not the voluntary causing of hurt, or the attempting to cause hurt` IS FALSE
    `for the purpose of preventing death or hurt` IS FALSE
    `not the abetment of an offence to which the exception would not extend` IS TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `exception under s 92` `hurting an insensible person to cure a minor ailment`

§§ `s 93`

-- s 93 illus: a surgeon, in good faith, tells a patient he cannot live; the
-- patient dies of the shock. No offence.
`the surgeon's grave prognosis` MEANS `Communication Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason of harm to the person to whom the communication is made` IS TRUE
    `the communication is made in good faith` IS TRUE
    `made for the benefit of that person` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `exception under s 93` `the surgeon's grave prognosis`

§§ `s 94`

-- s 94 Explanation 2: a smith seized by gang-robbers and forced by threat of
-- instant death to force a house door is entitled to the exception.
`the smith seized by gang-robbers` MEANS `Compulsion by Threats Facts` WITH
    `the offence is not murder` IS TRUE
    `the offence is not an offence against the State punishable with death` IS TRUE
    `compelled to do it by threats` IS TRUE
    `the threats, at the time of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension that instant death to that person or any other person will otherwise be the consequence` IS TRUE
    `did not of his own accord, or from a reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant death, place himself in the situation by which he became subject to such constraint` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `exception under s 94` `the smith seized by gang-robbers`

-- s 94 Explanation 1: one who joins gang-robbers of his own accord, or under a
-- threat of being beaten, knowing their character, is not.
`the gang-robbers' willing recruit` MEANS `Compulsion by Threats Facts` WITH
    `the offence is not murder` IS TRUE
    `the offence is not an offence against the State punishable with death` IS TRUE
    `compelled to do it by threats` IS TRUE
    `the threats, at the time of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension that instant death to that person or any other person will otherwise be the consequence` IS TRUE
    `did not of his own accord, or from a reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant death, place himself in the situation by which he became subject to such constraint` IS FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `exception under s 94` `the gang-robbers' willing recruit`

-- "Except murder": compulsion by threats of instant death does not excuse it.
`murder under threat of instant death` MEANS `Compulsion by Threats Facts` WITH
    `the offence is not murder` IS FALSE
    `the offence is not an offence against the State punishable with death` IS TRUE
    `compelled to do it by threats` IS TRUE
    `the threats, at the time of doing it, reasonably cause the apprehension that instant death to that person or any other person will otherwise be the consequence` IS TRUE
    `did not of his own accord, or from a reasonable apprehension of harm to himself short of instant death, place himself in the situation by which he became subject to such constraint` IS TRUE
#ASSERT NOT `exception under s 94` `murder under threat of instant death`

§§ `s 95`

`a trifling harm` MEANS `Slight Harm Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason that it causes, or is intended or known to be likely to cause, that harm` IS TRUE
    `that harm is so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of such harm` IS TRUE
#ASSERT `exception under s 95` `a trifling harm`

`a harm worth complaining of` MEANS `Slight Harm Facts` WITH
    `an offence, if at all, only by reason that it causes, or is intended or known to be likely to cause, that harm` IS TRUE
    `that harm is so slight that no person of ordinary sense and temper would complain of such harm` IS FALSE
#ASSERT NOT `exception under s 95` `a harm worth complaining of`

§ `Chapter 4A - tests`

§§ `ss 96-98, 101-103`

-- s 98 illus (a): Z ambushes A in a secluded area and attempts to kill him;
-- A kills Z while fending off the attack. A is guilty of no offence - he had
-- no reasonable opportunity to have recourse to a public authority.
`killing an ambusher in a secluded area` MEANS `Private Defence Facts` WITH
    `the act was done in defence against the act giving rise to the right` IS TRUE
    `the act defended against is an offence` IS TRUE
    `the act would be that offence but for the youth, want of maturity of understanding, unsoundness of mind or intoxication of the person doing it, or any misconception on his part` IS FALSE
    `the act falls under the definition of an offence affecting the human body, or is an attempt or a threat to commit one` IS TRUE
    `the act falls under the definition of theft, or is an attempt to commit theft` IS FALSE
    `the act falls under the definition of robbery, or is an attempt to commit robbery` IS FALSE
    `the act falls under the definition of mischief, or is an attempt to commit mischief` IS FALSE
    `the act falls under the definition of criminal trespass, or is an attempt to commit criminal trespass` IS FALSE
    `the criminal trespass is house-breaking` IS FALSE
    `the defender reasonably believed that there was danger to the body, his own or that of any other person, arising from that act` IS TRUE
    `that belief of danger continued when the act of defence was done` IS TRUE
    `the defender reasonably believed that there was a danger to property, his own or that of any other person, arising from that act` IS FALSE
    `the offender had not effected his retreat with the property` IS FALSE
    `the assistance of a public authority had not been obtained` IS FALSE
    `the property had not been recovered` IS FALSE
    `the offender was causing or attempting to cause to any person death or hurt or wrongful restraint` IS FALSE
    `the fear of instant death or of instant hurt or of instant personal restraint continued` IS FALSE
    `the offender continued in the commission of the criminal trespass or mischief` IS FALSE
    `the house-breaking continued` IS FALSE
    `the harm voluntarily caused to the assailant was harm other than death` IS FALSE
    `an assault where the defender reasonably believes that death will otherwise be the consequence of such assault` IS TRUE
    `an assault where the defender reasonably believes that grievous hurt will otherwise be the consequence of such assault` IS FALSE
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of committing rape as described in section 375 or causing such rape to be committed` IS FALSE
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of causing penile penetration of the vagina, anus or mouth as described in section 376(2)` IS FALSE
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of kidnapping or abducting` IS FALSE
    `an assault that the defender reasonably believes to be done with the intention of wrongfully confining a person, under circumstances which may reasonably cause him to believe that he will be unable to have opportunity for recourse to a public authority for his release` IS FALSE
    `the harm was caused to an innocent person, in running a risk of harm to an innocent person` IS FALSE
    `an assault which causes the defender to reasonably believe that death would be caused to him or to any other person` IS FALSE
    `the defender is so situated that he cannot effectually exercise that right without risk of harm to an innocent person` IS FALSE
    `the harm voluntarily caused to the wrongdoer was harm other than death` IS FALSE
    `the house-breaking was committed after 7 p.m. and before 7 a.m.` IS FALSE
    `the wrongdoer effected his entrance into the house or any part of it` IS FALSE
    `the wrongdoer, being in the house for the purpose of committing an offence or having committed an offence therein, leaves the house or any part of it` IS FALSE
    `enters or leaves through a passage made by the wrongdoer, or by any abettor of the house-breaking, in order to commit the house-breaking` IS FALSE
    `enters or leaves through a passage not intended for human entrance, or one reached by scaling or climbing over any wall or building` IS FALSE
    `enters or leaves through a passage opened, in order to commit the house-breaking, by means the occupier did not intend it to be opened by` IS FALSE
    `enters or leaves by opening any lock in order to commit the house-breaking, or to leave after it` IS FALSE
    `effects his entry or leaving by using criminal force or committing an assault, or by threatening any person with assault` IS FALSE
    `enters or leaves by a passage he knows to have been fastened against it, and to have been unfastened by himself or an abettor` IS FALSE
    `the mischief is mischief by fire committed on a building, tent, container or vessel used as a human dwelling, or as a place for the custody of property` IS FALSE
    `the defender reasonably believes that death or grievous hurt will be the consequence, if such right of private defence is not exercised` IS FALSE
    `the harm inflicted was no more than it was reasonably necessary to inflict in the circumstances` IS TRUE
    `there was no reasonable opportunity to have recourse to the protection of a public authority in the circumstances` IS TRUE
    `the act causes the defender to reasonably believe that death or grievous hurt would result` IS FALSE
    `the act is not done or attempted by a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of his office` IS TRUE
    `the defender did not know or have reason to believe that the person doing the act was such public servant` IS FALSE
    `the act is not done or attempted by the direction of a public servant acting in good faith under the actual or apparent authority of the public servant's office` IS TRUE
    `the defender did not know or have reason to believe that the person doing the act was acting by such direction` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act did not state the authority under which he acted` IS FALSE
    `the person doing the act had authority in writing and did not produce it when it was demanded` IS FALSE
#ASSERT `exception under s 96` `killing an ambusher in a secluded area`

